\b Relative locators \b0 :-\par
\par
Yes, relative locators were introduced in Selenium 4, also known as Selenium Grid 4. \par
Relative locators offer several strategies for locating elements relative to a reference element:\par
\par
\b near(): \b0 Locates an element near another element. You specify the reference element and the direction (to the left of, to the right of, above, below, or near) relative to it.\par
\par
\b above(): \b0 Locates an element above another element.\par
\par
\b below(): \b0 Locates an element below another element.\par
\par
\b toLeftOf(): \b0 Locates an element to the left of another element.\par
\par
\b toRightOf(): \b0 Locates an element to the right of another element.\par
 \par
        driver.get("{{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK https://www.example.com }}{\fldrslt{https://www.example.com\ul0\cf0}}}}\f0\fs22 ");\par
\par
        WebElement referenceElement = driver.findElement(By.id("referenceElementId"));\par
\par
        // Locate an element to the left of the reference element\par
        WebElement elementToLeft = driver.findElement(RelativeLocator.withTagName("div").toLeftOf(referenceElement));\par
\par
        // Locate an element below the reference element\par
        WebElement elementBelow = driver.findElement(RelativeLocator.withTagName("div").below(referenceElement));\par
\par
        // Perform actions on the located elements\par
        elementToLeft.click();\par
        elementBelow.sendKeys("Text input");\par
